\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Joint-embedding predictive architectures (JEPAs) learn a representation by predicting, in embedding space, the representation of a target part of an input from the representation of a context part~\citep{lecun2022path,assran2023ijepa,bardes2024vjepa}.
They use no negative examples and, unlike masked autoencoders~\citep{he2022mae}, reconstruct no pixels; the same objective, however, admits a trivial minimiser: if the encoder maps every input to the same point, every prediction is exact.
More subtly, the representation can collapse \emph{dimensionally}, spanning only a few directions of the embedding space~\citep{jing2022dimensional}, which degrades downstream performance~\citep{garrido2023rankme}.

JEPAs and their non-contrastive relatives~\citep{grill2020byol,chen2021simsiam,baevski2022data2vec,caron2021dino} avoid collapse through design choices: a predictor of a particular depth, width or learning rate; a high masking ratio and a geometry of context and target blocks; a stop-gradient on the target branch with an exponential moving average (EMA) of the online encoder.
Each choice is supported by ablations, and several have partial theoretical explanations~\citep{tian2021understanding,richemond2023edge,halvagal2023implicit,littwin2024jepa}, but there is no single account of \emph{why} these choices work, nor a criterion that predicts \emph{when} a configuration collapses and how much rank it keeps.

We build such an account on one premise: collapse is decided early in training, near the collapsed solution, where a \emph{driving force}, the predictive signal shared between context and target, grows each direction of representation space and a \emph{decay}, from weight decay and from the self-prediction pressure of the loss, shrinks it.
Recent work develops the same picture as a stability count at initialisation with the predictor held fixed~\citep{demelocosta2026drive}; we show that once the predictor is also decayed this count does not decide survival: the collapsed solution is locally stable and the predictor's gain decays, so survival is decided by a race between a direction's transient growth and the decay of the predictor, not by the sign of its growth rate at initialisation.

For linear BYOL/SimSiam and for predictive encoders with an optimal predictor, a mode survives weight decay only above a static threshold~\citep{tian2021understanding,wang2021directset,ponce2025dual,arbel2026peira}; those analyses take the predictor as small as the encoder, so the drive is quadratic at the collapsed point.
At a standard initialisation the predictor is of order one while the encoder is small; the drive is then first order, and survival depends on how far a direction grows before the predictor decays. Our contributions, relative to these analyses and to that stability count:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,nosep]
\item \textbf{The race criterion.} Near collapse, each data direction is carried by an amplitude and a predictor gain whose equations are known in substance (\cref{prop:reduction,prop:fixed}). In the unbalanced regime of a standard initialisation we derive when a direction captures the decaying gain before the gain leaves its growth window (\cref{def:race}); the criterion has no fitted constants and locates the separatrix between collapse and survival, which the static threshold does not.
\item \textbf{Predictions.} The criterion gives the rank, a critical weight decay, $\lambda_k^{\mathrm{race}}\approx h_k(p_0)/\log(p_0/a_0)$ for $\alpha=1$, far below the static threshold, a rank non-monotone in weight decay (rescue, then removal), survival from above the initial window once the decaying gain enters it, and the need for predictor weight decay whenever the encoder is decayed, and, beyond the count's doses at initialisation, how predictor learning rate and depth and target speed act (\cref{sec:heuristics}, \cref{tab:heuristics}).
\item \textbf{Tests.} On a development set and on \EoneBRuns{} held-out, pre-registered linear-JEPA runs (\cref{sec:experiments}), the criterion predicts the rank at convergence with error \EoneBAlignedMae{} against \EoneBAlignedWindowMae{} for the count of directions that grow at initialisation, which is that stability count in this model (stated as a bound, it is exceeded in \DvdAlignedOver{} of runs), and \EoneBAlignedBoundMae{} for the static threshold, and the collapse boundary in weight decay; we report where it fails (deep predictors, corrected post hoc and re-tested; one EMA sign; AdamW). Under masking it predicts the rank from the mask statistics with error \EtwoCycMae{} in population training (static: \EtwoCycBoundMae{}; SGD: \EtwoSgdMae{}) and fails for a tanh encoder under random masks. In a ViT on CIFAR-10 a step-0 rule orders the configurations of an SGD arm, through its weight-decay sweep, but misplaces the transition, and the predictor, EMA and masking effects do not transfer.
\end{itemize}
